          A fuel stabilizer is cheap insurance for a generator that waits months for the next outage. The right one depends on how long the fuel sits and how much you store, so this guide starts with what stabilizers do, then ranks six real products by job.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Fuel Stabilizer Does (and What It Cannot Do)
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best fuel stabilizer is the one that matches how long your gas will sit. A generator that waits months for the next outage needs a storage formula; a mower or generator you run every few weeks needs an in-season treatment. Both slow the same chemistry, at different strengths.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Gasoline Goes Bad</h3>

          <p>
            Gasoline is a blend of hydrocarbons, and the lighter ones evaporate first. As they go, the fuel becomes harder to ignite, which is why an engine on old gas cranks and cranks. At the same time, oxygen reacts with the fuel and forms gums and varnish. Those deposits settle in the narrowest passages, and in a small engine the narrowest passages are the carburetor jets. A partially blocked jet is the most common reason a generator surges, will not idle or will not start after storage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Ethanol Adds to the Problem</h3>

          <p>
            Most pump gas in the US is E10, up to 10 percent ethanol. Ethanol attracts moisture from the air. In a vented can or a half-empty tank, it slowly picks up water, and once there is enough, the ethanol and water separate from the gasoline and sink to the bottom, a process called phase separation. That bottom layer is what the fuel pickup draws first, and it burns poorly and promotes corrosion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Stabilizer Can and Cannot Do</h3>

          <p>
            A stabilizer adds antioxidants and, in most formulas, corrosion inhibitors. It slows oxidation, so the fuel stays combustible longer, and it protects metal parts. It does not reverse varnish that has already formed in a carburetor, it cannot turn water back into fuel once phase separation has happened, and it does not make old gas new. The time to add it is when the fuel is fresh, which is why the makers tell you to pour it into the can at the pump.
          </p>
        </div>

