        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Long Does Gas Last With a Fuel Stabilizer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the question most people are really asking, and the honest answer is a range that depends on the fuel, the container and where it is kept.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Untreated Gasoline</h3>

          <p>
            Untreated pump gas is commonly said to stay good for a few months at best in a sealed container kept cool, and as little as a month in a vented can through summer heat. Ethanol blends tend to be at the short end because of the moisture problem. That is why a generator filled in spring and left alone until a winter storm so often refuses to start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Treated Gasoline: What the Labels Claim</h3>

          <p>
            STA-BIL Storage's listing claims up to 24 months, and Sea Foam's claims up to two years. Those are maximums under good conditions, not guarantees for a vented can in a hot garage. Treat them as the outer limit and plan to rotate stored fuel well inside them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Shortens the Clock</h3>

          <p>
            Heat, air and moisture shorten fuel life. A full, sealed container in a cool, shaded spot lasts much longer than a half-empty one in the sun. Fuel that was already weeks old when you treated it starts with less life left. Add stabilizer on the day you buy the gas, not the day you put the generator away.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Practical Rotation Rule</h3>

          <p>
            Mark each can with the date you filled it. Every six months or so, pour the oldest can into your car's tank (a few gallons of treated fuel in a full car tank is a normal use) and refill it with fresh, treated gas. Rotation is how you make sure the fuel is good when the power is out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Use Fuel Stabilizer in a Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            The steps are the same for every product on this list. Only the dose changes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Treat the Can at the Pump</h3>

          <p>
            Pour the measured dose into the empty gas can before you fill it. The incoming fuel mixes it for you. STA-BIL's directions say the same for tanks: add the stabilizer first, then the gasoline.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Fill the Generator Tank With Treated Fuel</h3>

          <p>
            Top the generator's tank up with treated fuel rather than leaving it half full. Less air space means less moisture and less oxidation. Do this with the engine off and cool, and wipe up any spills before you start it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Run the Engine for About Five Minutes</h3>

          <p>
            STA-BIL's directions call for running the engine for about five minutes after adding treated fuel so it reaches the carburetor and fuel lines. This step is easy to skip and important: untreated fuel left in the carburetor bowl is the fuel most likely to varnish.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Store It and Exercise It</h3>

          <p>
            Close the fuel valve if your generator has one and store it as the owner's manual describes; our guide to <a href="/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/">storing a portable generator</a> covers covers, batteries and oil. Many owners run the generator under a small load every month or two, which keeps fresh fuel moving through the carburetor and confirms it will start when you need it. Fresh oil matters as much as fresh fuel before storage, and our <a href="/best-generator-oil/">generator oil</a> guide matches 10W-30, 5W-30 and SAE 30 to the temperatures you will run in.
          </p>
        </div>

