        <div id="ethanol" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Ethanol Gas and Small Engines: What to Do About E10
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ethanol is the main reason small engines now need more care in storage than they did a generation ago. You cannot always avoid it, but you can manage it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Phase Separation</h3>

          <p>
            When ethanol-blended fuel takes on enough water, the ethanol and water drop out together as a separate layer. You may see it as a cloudy band at the bottom of a clear container. A stabilizer helps by slowing the conditions that lead there, but it cannot recombine fuel that has already separated. Fuel in that state should not go into an engine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ethanol-Free Gas</h3>

          <p>
            Some stations sell ethanol-free gasoline, often marked as recreational or marine fuel, and canned ethanol-free fuel is sold for small engines. It removes the moisture problem and is the easiest option for equipment that sits a long time. It still oxidizes, though, so it still benefits from a stabilizer when stored for months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Marine or Ethanol Formula Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            If your generator lives in a damp garage, a boathouse or a coastal climate, a formula aimed at ethanol and moisture, such as STA-BIL 360 Marine or Lucas Safeguard, is worth the extra cost for fuel you burn through the season. Pair it with a storage stabilizer when the fuel will sit for months.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Storing Gas for a Power Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            A <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator for power outages</a> is only as good as the fuel next to it. Stored gas is where most outage plans fail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Containers</h3>

          <p>
            Use approved gas cans with intact seals, and keep them full rather than half empty. Store them away from the house, out of sun and away from anything that sparks or has a pilot light, such as a water heater or furnace. Check your local fire code: many places limit how much gasoline you can store at home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much to Keep</h3>

          <p>
            Work it out from your generator's run time. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-can-a-generator-run-continuously/">how long a generator can run continuously</a> shows how to estimate fuel use per day. Multiply by the number of days you want to cover, and keep that amount treated and rotated rather than a larger stock you never use. If you are still choosing the machine itself, our <a href="/best-gas-generator/">best gas generator</a> rankings compare tank size and run time for each pick.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Alternatives to Storing Gas</h3>

          <p>
            A <a href="/best-dual-fuel-inverter-generator/">dual fuel generator</a> can also run on propane, which does not go stale in a tank, and a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a> needs no fuel at all for smaller loads. Many households keep both: propane or a battery for the first day, and treated gas for longer outages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="old-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Can a Fuel Stabilizer Fix Old Gas?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some labels suggest it can. PRI-G's listing says it can rejuvenate degraded gasoline so it is usable again. Here is how to decide whether to try.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs Gas Has Gone Bad</h3>

          <p>
            Fresh gasoline is clear and smells sharp. Old fuel turns darker, smells sour or like varnish, and may show sediment or a separate layer at the bottom. An engine that starts and then surges, or runs only with the choke on, is often telling you the same thing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Treatment Is Worth Trying</h3>

          <p>
            If the fuel is a few months past its best, still clear and not separated, adding a stabilizer and mixing it with fresh gas is a reasonable step for a lawn mower or a car's tank. For a generator you depend on in an emergency, the cost of a few gallons of fresh fuel is small next to the cost of a failed start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Dispose of It</h3>

          <p>
            Fuel that is dark, separated or full of particles should go to a household hazardous waste site. Never pour it on the ground or down a drain. Drain it from the generator's tank and carburetor bowl as the owner's manual describes, refill with fresh treated fuel, and run the engine before you put it away again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-engines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Fuel Stabilizer for Mowers, Snow Blowers and Other Engines
          </h2>

          <p>
            The same bottle works across most of the small engines in a garage, which is a good reason to buy one size up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lawn Mowers</h3>

          <p>
            At the end of the mowing season, treat the last tank and run the engine so treated fuel fills the carburetor. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-winterize-a-lawn-mower/">winterizing a lawn mower</a> walks through the whole routine, including oil and the blade.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Snow Blowers</h3>

          <p>
            Snow blowers have the reverse calendar: they sit all summer. Treat the fuel before the last storm of the winter, and see <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-snow-blower-for-winter/">how to prepare a snow blower for winter</a> for the start-of-season checks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Stroke Tools</h3>

          <p>
            For chainsaws and trimmers, add stabilizer to the gasoline before mixing in the two-stroke oil, and mix only what you will use in a month or two. Lucas Safeguard and Sea Foam both list 2-cycle engines among their uses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Stabilizer to Gas That Is Already Old</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stabilizer slows oxidation in fresh fuel; it does not reverse it. Treating a can that has sat in the garage since spring only preserves fuel that has already started to go stale. Add it at the pump, on the day you buy the gas, and date the can.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Five-Minute Run</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Treated fuel in the tank does nothing for the carburetor bowl, which is where varnish forms first. STA-BIL's directions call for running the engine for about five minutes after adding treated fuel. Skip it and the fuel most likely to cause a no-start is the one left untreated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Guessing the Dose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Doses vary from 1 ounce per 2.5 gallons to about 1 ounce per 16 gallons. Pouring a concentrate like a storage formula wastes product, and pouring a storage formula like a concentrate underdoses the fuel. Read the label once, then write the dose for your can size on the can.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an In-Season Treatment for Long Storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                STA-BIL says its 360 Marine treatment is for in-season use and points buyers to STA-BIL Storage for long storage. If fuel will sit for six months or more, use a product made for storage or pair the two, as the maker recommends.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Fuel and Never Rotating It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even treated gasoline has a limit, and the labels give maximums under good conditions. A can that has sat for three years is a gamble in an emergency. Use the oldest can in your car about every six months and refill it with fresh, treated fuel.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

