        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best fuel stabilizer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a generator or small engine that sits between uses, STA-BIL Storage is the best fuel stabilizer for most people. The listing claims fuel stays fresh for up to 24 months at 1 ounce per 2.5 gallons, and the dose is easy to pour. PRI-G is the better choice for large fuel reserves, and Sea Foam suits anyone who wants a stabilizer that also cleans.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does gas last with a fuel stabilizer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                STA-BIL Storage's listing claims up to 24 months and Sea Foam's up to two years, under good storage conditions. In real garages with vented cans and summer heat, plan on less. Our <a href="/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/">generator storage guide</a> plans on three to six months of reliable life even with stabilizer, so a practical rule is to rotate treated gas about every six months by using the oldest can in your car and refilling it with fresh, treated fuel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much fuel stabilizer do I put in 5 gallons of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product. STA-BIL Storage needs 2 ounces per 5 gallons, Lucas Safeguard about 1 ounce, STA-BIL 360 Marine half an ounce, and the concentrates PRI-G and Star Tron about a third of an ounce. Always follow the label of the bottle you have, because doses differ by as much as six times.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a fuel stabilizer for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the generator sits for more than a month or two between runs, yes. Gasoline oxidizes and ethanol blends absorb moisture, and the deposits that form clog small carburetor jets. A stabilizer added to fresh fuel, plus a five-minute run to get it into the carburetor, is the cheapest insurance that the generator starts during an outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put fuel stabilizer in old gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it will not make bad fuel good. Stabilizer slows oxidation in fresh fuel; it cannot undo varnish or recombine fuel that has separated. If old gas is still clear, mixing it with fresh treated fuel in a car tank is reasonable. If it is dark, sour-smelling or layered, take it to a hazardous waste site.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Sea Foam a fuel stabilizer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Sea Foam's listing describes it as a fuel stabilizer that keeps gas or diesel fresh for up to two years, as well as a fuel system cleaner and oil treatment. It is a good all-round choice, though dedicated storage products such as STA-BIL Storage and PRI-G are more specific about storage and, for PRI-G, more concentrated.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I drain the gas or use a stabilizer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check your owner's manual first, because some makers recommend running the carburetor dry for long storage and others recommend a full tank of stabilized fuel. For a generator you may need at short notice, a full tank of treated fuel is usually more practical, because it is ready to start. Either way, never leave untreated fuel sitting in the carburetor.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

